Z||000006||Jncomutt||12-01-2001||07:13 PM||||I got a pair from the Fiero Store.  I returned my exhaust from them and just swapped to those.  Don't be fooled, they're not as easily as one would thing.  If you look at your gauges, you'll notice the black metal face is sort of depressed from where the tabs are that mount it.  Well the fiero store gauges, while they are the exact shape as the metal ones, they don't have the depression.  So if you try to mount them using the hole, you'll notice that the needle won't fit on.<P>What I did was use an exacto knife to cut off the tabs.  I then popped off the rivets that hold the metal face to the gauge itself.  I then found screws that would fit the holes of the rivets.  Well the gauge faces did come with the holes that match up to these precut so I just laid it on top and used the screws to hold it in place.  I was able to do this for the speedometer and the Tach.  Thing about the fuel gauge one is that the metal face was used to hold the 2 separate gauges in place..  I tried to use the abouve method anyways but found that when i hit a bump the gauges would get misaligned with the screw clip things on the back and the gauge would no longer be centerd in the face.  I managed to cut the face in a way that its quite snug and doesn't seem to move around but I would consider it just a patch for now, not really a fix.<P>The gauges are great.  They look awesome in the day.  They are bright and they are reflective.  When the sun hits em, the shine soo well.  At night they are much better as well.. The light comes through them instead of around and glows BRIGHT red.  The become much brighter than how the stock setup was.  <P>All in all, I like em.  I would have preferred if they weren't $100, I don't think its worth that much, but after the not so easy install, they're different, and easier to read.  Hope it helps, sorry if its wordy.   ||||reg||1
